{"kind":"artifact","text":"The fold reads a retirement for what its own act rested on. A supersession resting on an artifact covering the same path - the same string, or a directory containing it - is succession: Artifact.Succeeded is set, and that retirement carries no staleness across reasoning edges, so the approval, the report, the promise, the request and the commitment of a completed loop no longer flare on the merge that completed them. A supersession naming no covering successor is condemnation and propagates exactly as before. Artifact-to-artifact provenance carries the flare either way, so a page describing an implementation still goes stale and still reads describes_superseded_world when that implementation is replaced. ProfileVersion advances to workroom-fold@6. gs status --all now renders SUCCEEDED - replaced at the same path apart from RETIRED - withdrawn with no successor. Tests pin all three directions in internal/workroom/fold_test.go: TestSuccessionLeavesTheCompletedLoopUnflared, TestRetirementWithoutASuccessorStillFlaresTheLoop, TestLaterArtifactAtTheSamePathDoesNotRescueACondemnedOne, TestSuccessionStillFlaresTheDocumentationAboveIt, TestSuccessionFollowsOnlyACoveringSuccessor, TestRefusedSupersessionRecordsNoSuccession. Each was mutation-checked: forcing succession never to rescue, always to rescue, and to rescue across artifact provenance each fails a different test. Gates at this exact head: go build, go vet, gofmt all clean; go test -count=1 ./... green in 26 packages; go test -race green for workroom, wireparity and statusview with no data races. internal/docset fails at this head with exactly the five citations that already fail on origin/main ca2836ab, verified by running the same gate at that commit and diffing the sets - it is pre-existing and untouched by this change.","body":{"commit":"14fdfb2b434105a878ca5173f2d0f8deff60184d","path":"internal/workroom"}}